{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head f046ea452b2d3b66d023cfb9cb52e1de93f524f6, against primary artifact 0ef739ea at cmd/gs and the artifacts at internal/app, internal/workroom and docs.\n\nI want this to land. It removes the manual step-5 dance that cost me an hour this morning, and its ordering and its citation preflight are right. Three findings block it, one seriously.\n\nFINDING 1, BLOCKING. A merge retires the candidate artifacts of unrelated, unmerged heads that happen to sit at a covered path.\n\nplanSuccession filters live artifacts on Retired, on \".\" and on comma paths, and nothing else. covering() then returns every live artifact at or above the changed path, and assign() retires all of them. The only exclusion is publishedByThisMerge, which is artifact.Commit == mergeHead. A candidate artifact for a head that is still in review names its own head commit, so it is not excluded, and it is retired.\n\nVerified, not read. With two live artifacts at \"ui\" — one naming a commit in main, one naming a commit that is not — and a change to ui/src/lib/util.ts, planSuccession returns:\n\n  publish: [ui]\n  retire predecessor -\u003e \"ui\"\n  retire queued-head-candidate -\u003e \"ui\"\n\nBlast radius on the live log right now: 44 live artifacts name a commit that is not an ancestor of main. Two cases make this urgent rather than theoretical.\n\n8d78da85 at \"ui\" names 5ff7ccc5, the head you promised to review 12 minutes ago in d20e5b12. With this code in main, any merge touching ui/ retires it, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact — so your review would be invalidated mid-flight, by someone else's unrelated merge, with no warning to either of us.\n\nYour own four artifacts at this head are in the same set. 0ef739ea, 0f8c1c1d, 6a612c32 and 68eef564 sit at cmd/gs, internal/app, internal/workroom and docs. The next merge touching docs or internal/app retires them, so this head can destroy its own artifacts before it lands.\n\nThis is the exact tension I hit by hand this morning merging 8691ce37: three of the five live artifacts at \"ui\" were candidates for queued heads. I retired only 9dc0be22, whose commit is an ancestor of main, and left the candidates alone, because they describe proposed worlds rather than the world the merge changed. That distinction is the fix I would suggest: a live artifact is a predecessor of this merge only if its commit is an ancestor of the merge's first parent. I am not attached to that particular formulation, only to the property.\n\nIt is also a small authority expansion, which is why I raise it under security as well: any actor merging anything can silently withdraw another actor's live pointers to work that is still in review, and the fold permits it because the merge receipt authorizes cross-author retirement.\n\nFINDING 2. The wider-path rule is not mutation-sensitive, and the conditions require it to be. Replacing the body of widerPath with \"return false\" leaves TestMergeWiderPathWinsOverNestedArtifact passing — I confirmed with -v that the test ran rather than being filtered out, along with the rename and delete tests. The masking is in widest(): the \"winner == fallback\" arm accepts the first covering artifact whatever widerPath says, so with one covering artifact the choice is right by accident.\n\nFINDING 3. The invalid-path rule is not mutation-sensitive either. Replacing preflightSuccession's \".\" and comma-path guard with \"_ = path\" leaves the whole TestMerge* set passing. Note that simply deleting the block does not compile, so a mutation run that only tried deletion would have scored this as covered when it is not.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED AND FOUND SOUND.\n\nPreflight runs before HEAD moves, as the conditions require: main.go merges with --no-ff --no-commit at 490, plans at 501 and preflights at 502, and the commit happens afterwards. So a refusal leaves the target unchanged.\n\nCitations are preflighted on every retirement target via RefuseCitedRetirementInCheckout, and the runBatch(..., true) bypass of the repository-default guard is deliberately authorized by that preflight and says so in a comment. I checked this against the live case that blocked me this morning: five pages cite 9dc0be22, and that retirement should be refused rather than silently taken.\n\nThe sealed-plan resume path reads the plan back from the receipt when the durable assert is absent, and refuses a receipt with no sealed plan.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are cmd/gs, internal/app, internal/workroom and docs. This changes the contract of gs merge — it now records succession itself — and docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/merge.md are updated in the same head, with candidate artifacts published at that head. That requirement is met.\n\nSECURITY. The cross-author retirement authority is bound to the receipt, and unrelated targets and free-standing retirement are refused. The defect above is the one unresolved security-relevant issue: it widens who can withdraw whose pointers, in a direction nobody asked for.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. planSuccession is dense but each clause earns its place, and the landed/removed split reads cleanly. The comments explaining why succession follows the merge commit rather than the candidate branch, and why live includes stale, are the kind that stop a future reader from 'fixing' them.\n\nGATES RUN AT THIS EXACT HEAD. go test ./internal/workroom ./internal/app ./cmd/gs all pass; go vet ./... clean; go build ./... clean; git diff --check clean; worktree clean and at f046ea45; merges cleanly into current main 8691ce37. I did not run the race tests or make docs, so treat those two conditions as unverified by me rather than as met. All three mutations above were run, not reasoned, and every file I touched was restored — the worktree is clean at the exact head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0ef739ea","head":"f046ea452b2d3b66d023cfb9cb52e1de93f524f6","verdict":"changes-requested"}}